nodecode-ram-first-context is a rewrite of ether-btc's ether-btc/ram-first-context:

  https://github.com/ether-btc/ram-first-context
  commit 364992afde615e8588e285fbb4dd6fe8d57f6869
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 ether-btc (LICENSE)

The original is a Hermes Agent plugin: one Python module that registered
mnemosyne_fts_recall, a read-only FTS5 keyword search over the Mnemosyne
database the harness's memory provider keeps, with a LIKE fallback when the
index or the file cannot answer, an opt-in local usage counter, and every
answer -- errors included -- a JSON array. The rewrite keeps that tool under
the same name, with the same two arguments and the same answer: the same two
statements, term quoting that makes FTS5 operators and punctuation literal,
the trailing-star prefix, the LIKE fallback ordered by importance, the error
record instead of an exception, and the opt-in local usage line.

What it left out is what belonged to the other harness: the plugin's
plugin.yaml manifest and its packaging (the entry-point install path, its CI
and its pytest suite), Hermes's own profile resolution, and the loader health
check that hid the tool while the database was absent -- in Nodecode a search
answers the error record instead, and the section's db_path is the operator's
to name. The connection is opened with PRAGMA query_only rather than the
original's read-only URI, because Nodecode's SQLite binding opens by path;
the statements are still SELECTs only. The original's telemetry was an
environment flag; here it is the section's usage_log, off by default. Nothing
of the original is bundled here, and nothing of it runs: the search is Lisp
over the same SQLite file.
